how the hell do these writes work:

<a> will they allocate cache lines just not do the read cacheline part 

<b> bypass cache hit write combine buffer and then flush to main memory

<c> pass through LLC dont allocate cache line but do write invalidate (buffer in WC)

I suspect its <c>. That way they re 
-> coherent with writeback stores
-> use WC so are not completely limitted by memory bandwidth
-> most importantly avoid cache line pollution


I __suspect__ the primary goal is to avoid cacheline pollution / be coherent and get
as much write speed up using WC as possible.

If write speed was all that mattered they could have used the LLC as a Write combined buffer.

Speed up also happens because the one instruction does 128B at a time (SSE) so instruction cost if any 
